# Local environment files must never be uploaded with a deployment.
#
# Vercel injects the project's own environment variables at runtime, and a
# .env travelling with the source is loaded by Next on top of them — so a
# value set for one developer's laptop silently overrides the deployment's.
#
# This is not hypothetical. A local `AUTH_CODES_DIR=.data/sign-in`, written for
# file-sink testing, shipped to a preview deployment and took priority over
# AUTH_CODES_INLINE. sinkFromEnv() checks the directory first, so it built a
# FileSink on a read-only filesystem, reported "not configured", and every
# sign-in returned 503 — with the deployment's own AUTH_CODES_INLINE=1 sitting
# correct and unread. It cost four wrong theories to find, because the
# variable being wrong was invisible from the Vercel side: `vercel env ls`
# showed exactly the two variables that were intended.
#
# .gitignore does list .env, and the CLI did not exclude it on that basis.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Local database and delivered sign-in codes
.data
